(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-1-anatomy-master-exam-form-d"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form D","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"In which planes do flexion and extension usually occur?","choices":["Only the transverse plane","Midsagittal or parasagittal planes","Only the midcoronal plane","No specific plane at all"],"answer":1,"exp":"Flexion and extension usually occur in the midsagittal or parasagittal planes. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the difference between flexion and extension?","choices":["Flexion moves away from the ventral surface; extension brings ventral surfaces together","Both describe the same movement at different joints","Flexion and extension both describe rotation about a vertical axis","Flexion brings ventral surfaces together; extension moves away from the ventral surface"],"answer":3,"exp":"Flexion brings primitively ventral surfaces together (e.g., bending the arm at the elbow), while extension is movement away from the ventral surface (e.g., straightening the leg at the knee joint). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is plantar flexion versus dorsiflexion of the foot?","choices":["Plantar flexion occurs at the knee joint rather than at the ankle joint itself","Plantar flexion is described as upward flexion of the foot; dorsiflexion is downward flexion of the foot","Both terms are described as referring to the exact same movement occurring at the ankle joint","Plantar flexion is downward flexion (true flexion) at the ankle; dorsiflexion is upward flexion (extension) at the ankle"],"answer":3,"exp":"Plantar flexion is downward flexion (true flexion) of the foot at the ankle joint, while dorsiflexion is upward flexion (extension) of the foot at the ankle joint. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"In which plane do abduction and adduction usually occur?","choices":["The midsagittal plane","The transverse plane","No specific plane","The midcoronal plane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Abduction and adduction usually occur in the midcoronal plane. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is the difference between abduction and adduction?","choices":["Abduction is movement away from the median; adduction is movement toward the median","Abduction only applies to the wrist; adduction only applies to the ankle","Both describe rotation about a vertical axis","Abduction is movement toward the median; adduction is movement away from the median"],"answer":0,"exp":"Abduction (lateral flexion) is movement away from the median (away from the middle finger/2nd toe), while adduction is movement toward the median (toward the middle finger/2nd toe). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What are radial deviation and ulnar deviation, as specific examples of abduction/adduction at the wrist?","choices":["Radial deviation is abduction of the hand at the wrist; ulnar deviation is adduction of the hand at the wrist","Both radial and ulnar deviation occur at the elbow joint rather than at the wrist","Radial deviation is adduction of the hand; ulnar deviation is abduction of the hand","Both terms describe the exact same movement occurring at the wrist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Radial deviation is abduction of the hand at the wrist joint, and ulnar deviation is adduction of the hand at the wrist joint. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"About what axis do medial and lateral rotation usually occur?","choices":["A vertical axis","The axis of the sagittal plane only","A horizontal axis in the coronal plane","No fixed axis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Medial rotation and lateral rotation usually occur about a vertical axis. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the difference between medial rotation and lateral rotation?","choices":["Both describe the same rotational movement","Medial and lateral rotation only apply to the ankle","Medial rotation moves the ventral surface away from the median; lateral rotation moves it toward the median","Medial rotation moves the ventral surface toward the median; lateral rotation moves it away from the median"],"answer":3,"exp":"Medial rotation is movement of the ventral surface toward the median (e.g., bringing a flexed arm across the chest), while lateral rotation is movement of the ventral surface away from the median (e.g., directing the head toward one side). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is the difference between pronation and supination of the hand?","choices":["Pronation is medial rotation so the palm faces posteriorly; supination is lateral rotation so the palm faces anteriorly","Pronation is lateral rotation so the palm faces anteriorly; supination is medial rotation so the palm faces posteriorly","Pronation and supination describe movements of the foot, not the hand","Both describe the same wrist movement"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pronation is medial rotation so the palm faces posteriorly, while supination is lateral rotation so the palm faces anteriorly (as when holding a cup of soup). — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the difference between inversion and eversion of the foot?","choices":["Inversion rotates the plantar surface laterally; eversion rotates it inward","Both describe the same foot movement","Inversion and eversion describe movements of the hand, not the foot","Inversion rotates the plantar surface inward; eversion rotates it laterally"],"answer":3,"exp":"Inversion rotates the plantar (sole) surface inward, while eversion rotates the plantar surface laterally. — Introduction to Anatomy & Anatomical Terminology - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What are the two main components of cartilage matrix, and is cartilage vascular?","choices":["Mostly fibers and ground substance; cartilage is avascular and not innervated","Composed mostly of minerals; cartilage tissue is described as highly vascularized","Mostly fluid; cartilage heals rapidly thanks to an abundant blood supply","Mostly cells; cartilage is richly innervated with pain and pressure fibers"],"answer":0,"exp":"Cartilage matrix is mostly fibers and ground substance, and cartilage is avascular and not innervated, making it slow to heal. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the perichondrium, and what limits cartilage thickness?","choices":["Surrounding tissue from which nutrients diffuse and chondrocytes form; diffusion must cross the entire thickness, limiting how thick cartilage can be","A vascular membrane that directly supplies blood to embedded chondrocytes within the matrix","A mineral layer embedded within the matrix that actively prevents any further outward cartilage growth from occurring","A bony covering surrounding and reinforcing the cartilage matrix from the outside, continuous with periosteum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The perichondrium is the surrounding tissue from which nutrients diffuse and where chondrocytes form; because diffusion must cross the entire thickness of the cartilage, this limits how thick cartilage can become. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are the three types of cartilage described in the material?","choices":["Compact, spongy, and reticular","Hyaline, fibrocartilage, and elastic","Dense regular, dense irregular, and areolar","Simple, stratified, and transitional"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three types of cartilage are hyaline, fibrocartilage, and elastic. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is hyaline cartilage's structure and where is it found?","choices":["Contains mostly elastin fibers, giving it recoil; forms the pinna of the ear and the epiglottis","Mostly mineralized ground substance similar to bone; found exclusively within the flat bones of the skull","Tough and flexible with more matrix/fewer cells, serving as a shock absorber; covers ends of long bones and forms the \"skeleton\" of the trachea and bronchi","Less firm with more cells and fibers, resembling dense connective tissue; forms the intervertebral disks"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hyaline cartilage is tough and flexible with more matrix and fewer cells, serving as a shock absorber; it covers the ends of long bones as articular cartilage and forms the \"skeleton\" of the trachea and bronchi. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is fibrocartilage's structure and where is it found?","choices":["Contains mostly elastin fibers with high recoil; forms the pinna of the ear and the epiglottis","The most abundant cartilage type in the body, forming the tracheal and bronchial skeletal framework","Less firm than hyaline, with more cells and fibers, similar in structure to dense regular tissue (tendon); forms intervertebral disks and knee menisci","Tough and flexible, acting as the primary shock absorber within joints; it covers the articulating ends of long bones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Fibrocartilage is less firm than hyaline cartilage, with more cells and fibers, structurally similar to dense regular tissue (tendon); it forms a transitional tissue between tendon and articular hyaline cartilage, and forms the intervertebral disks and spongy knee menisci. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What is elastic cartilage's composition and where is it found?","choices":["Contains mostly reticular fibers; forms lymph nodes","Contains mostly calcium hydroxyapatite; forms the vertebrae","Contains more elastin fibers; forms the ear pinna and epiglottis","Contains mostly ground substance with few fibers; forms the trachea"],"answer":2,"exp":"Elastic cartilage contains more elastin fibers than the other cartilage types, and forms structures like the ear pinna and the epiglottis. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which type of cartilage is described as the most abundant type in the body?","choices":["Reticular cartilage","Hyaline cartilage","Fibrocartilage","Elastic cartilage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Hyaline cartilage is described as the most abundant type of cartilage in the body. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What does bone matrix mostly consist of, and what percentage of bone weight is calcium hydroxyapatite?","choices":["Mostly calcium and phosphate; 65% of bone weight is calcium hydroxyapatite","Composed mostly of collagen protein; roughly 65% of bone weight is attributed to collagen fibers","Mostly water; bone contains no hydroxyapatite","Mostly ground substance; 25% of bone weight is hydroxyapatite"],"answer":0,"exp":"Bone matrix is mostly calcium and phosphate, and 65% of bone weight is calcium hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate, calcium hydroxide, calcium carbonate). — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Is bone tissue vascular and innervated, unlike cartilage?","choices":["Yes — bone is highly vascular and well innervated, and contains lymph channels","No — bone is avascular and uninnervated, like cartilage","Bone is vascular but not innervated, unlike the surrounding cartilage tissue","Bone is innervated but avascular, unlike the surrounding cartilage tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Unlike cartilage, bone is highly vascular and well innervated, and it contains lymph channels. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are the two functions of bone tissue related to mineral and blood, per the material?","choices":["Blood cell production and lubrication of joints","Mineral storage and impulse transmission","Fat digestion and mineral storage","Mineral storage and blood cell production"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bone tissue functions in mineral storage and blood cell production. — Histology for Posting-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are arachnoid granulations, and what is their function?","choices":["Blood vessels that specifically supply the choroid plexus with arterial blood flow","Projections of the arachnoid mater through which CSF flows to enter blood circulation","Clusters of autonomic nuclei located deep within the medulla oblongata itself","Folds of the dura mater that separate the two cerebral hemispheres from each other"],"answer":1,"exp":"Arachnoid granulations are projections of the arachnoid mater through which CSF flows to enter into blood circulation. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What underlies the arachnoid layer, and what is its function?","choices":["The choroid plexus, which actively produces cerebrospinal fluid","Weblike collagenous material that helps anchor cerebral blood vessels","The dural sinus, which primarily drains venous blood away from the brain","A rigid layer of bone that lies directly beneath the overlying skull"],"answer":1,"exp":"Underlying the arachnoid layer is weblike material consisting of collagenous fibers that help anchor cerebral blood vessels. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What does the pia mater do, and what does it follow?","choices":["Lines the inside of the skull bones only, forming the outer periosteal layer of the dura mater and playing no other role","Forms a loose, weblike covering that sits separate from the brain surface, bridging directly over the sulci rather than dipping into them","Attached to the surface of the brain, cerebellum, and spinal cord; follows the sulci and gyri of the brain and helps anchor larger cerebral blood vessels","Produces cerebrospinal fluid within the ventricles via the choroid plexus tissue, and never directly touches the brain's surface"],"answer":2,"exp":"The pia mater is attached to the surface of the brain, cerebellum, and spinal cord, following the sulci and gyri of the brain, and helps anchor the larger blood vessels of the cerebrum. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What are the functions of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), and where is it produced?","choices":["Regulates body temperature and systemic blood pressure; produced entirely by the dura mater's inner layer","Transports oxygen and glucose directly to neurons; produced entirely by the arachnoid granulations themselves","Provides protection, support, and nutrients to the CNS and transports waste away; produced by ependymal cells of the choroid plexus","Provides only structural support, with no nutrient or waste-clearance role at all; produced entirely by the pia mater"],"answer":2,"exp":"CSF provides protection, support, and nutrients to the CNS and transports waste away from the CNS; it is produced by the ependymal cells of the choroid plexus. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the correct order of CSF circulation, starting from the choroid plexus of the lateral ventricles?","choices":["Lateral ventricles → intraventricular foramen → 3rd ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → 4th ventricle → subarachnoid space → arachnoid granulations/villi","Subarachnoid space → 4th ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → 3rd ventricle → lateral ventricles → central canal, in reverse","4th ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → 3rd ventricle → intraventricular foramen → lateral ventricles → central canal only","Lateral ventricles → subarachnoid space directly, entirely bypassing the 3rd and 4th ventricles and cerebral aqueduct"],"answer":0,"exp":"CSF circulates from the choroid plexus of the lateral ventricles, through the intraventricular foramen, into the 3rd ventricle, through the cerebral aqueduct, into the 4th ventricle, then through the lateral and median apertures and central canal into the subarachnoid space, eventually entering circulation via the arachnoid granulations and villi. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What structural feature of brain blood vessels creates the blood-brain barrier?","choices":["A thick layer of dense bone directly surrounding the vessels","A near-total absence of capillaries within brain tissue","Tight endothelial cell junctions lining the blood vessels","A thick layer of adipose tissue surrounding each vessel"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lining of brain blood vessels consists of tight endothelial cell junctions, which create the blood-brain barrier. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What types of materials can pass through the blood-brain barrier, and how?","choices":["Both lipid- and water-soluble materials pass freely through the barrier without any restriction at all","No materials of any kind, whether lipid- or water-soluble, can ever pass through the blood-brain barrier","Only water-soluble materials can pass, and they do so freely without any transport mechanisms needed","Lipid-soluble materials pass through directly; water-soluble materials pass only via specific transport mechanisms"],"answer":3,"exp":"Due to the tight endothelial junctions, only lipid-soluble materials can pass through the blood-brain barrier directly, while water-soluble material can only pass via specific transport mechanisms. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What does \"autonomic\" mean, and what functions does the ANS regulate?","choices":["\"Automatic,\" but its entire regulatory role is limited strictly to controlling heart rate and rhythm alone","\"Automatic,\" outside conscious awareness; regulates body temperature, digestion, reproductive functions, breathing, and heart beat","\"Voluntary,\" though still technically outside conscious awareness; regulates only digestive processes exclusively","\"Automatic,\" yet fully under conscious voluntary control; regulates only skeletal muscle movement and posture"],"answer":1,"exp":"Autonomic means \"automatic\" — outside of our conscious awareness — and the ANS regulates functions like body temperature, digestion, reproductive functions, breathing, and heart beat. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"How do autonomic efferent neurons differ from somatic efferent nerves in terms of ganglia?","choices":["Neither type of efferent neuron involves ganglia at all; both somatic and autonomic fibers travel directly from the CNS to their target tissue without any intervening synapse whatsoever","Autonomic efferent neurons innervate skeletal muscle directly with no ganglia involved; somatic efferent nerves innervate visceral organs and synapse in ganglia located outside the CNS","Autonomic efferent neurons innervate visceral organs and have ganglionic neurons that synapse in ganglia outside the CNS; somatic efferent nerves innervate skeletal muscle and do not involve ganglia","Both types of efferent neurons synapse identically in ganglia located just outside the spinal cord, with no meaningful functional distinction existing between the two systems at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Autonomic efferent neurons innervate visceral organs and have ganglionic neurons that synapse in ganglia outside the CNS, while somatic efferent nerves innervate skeletal muscle and do not involve ganglia. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What are the three divisions of the autonomic nervous system?","choices":["Sympathetic, somatic, and parasympathetic, with no enteric division","Craniosacral, thoracolumbar, and somatic, with enteric excluded entirely","Only two total divisions exist: sympathetic and parasympathetic alone","Sympathetic (thoracolumbar), parasympathetic (craniosacral), and enteric"],"answer":3,"exp":"The three divisions of the autonomic nervous system are the sympathetic (thoracolumbar division), parasympathetic (craniosacral division), and enteric nervous system. — 3. Brain and ANS 2026.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What paired arteries supply blood to the brain?","choices":["External carotid and subclavian arteries","Only the vertebral arteries","Internal carotid and vertebral arteries","Only the internal carotid arteries"],"answer":2,"exp":"The brain receives blood from the paired internal carotid and vertebral arteries. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where does the internal carotid artery (ICA) branch from, and how does it enter the skull?","choices":["Arises as a branch of the subclavian artery, entering the skull through the foramen magnum","Arises directly as a branch of the aorta, entering the skull through the jugular foramen","Branches from the common carotid artery in the neck, entering through the carotid canal","Arises as a branch of the vertebral artery, entering the skull through the optic canal"],"answer":2,"exp":"The internal carotid artery (ICA) branches from the common carotid artery in the neck and enters the skull through the carotid canal. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Where do the vertebral arteries branch from, and what path do they take to enter the skull?","choices":["Arise directly as branches of the aorta, passing through the jugular foramen into the skull","Arise as branches of the common carotid artery, passing through the carotid canal into the skull","Branch from the subclavian artery, passing through the transverse foramina of C1-C6 and through the foramen magnum","Branch from the internal carotid artery, passing through the optic canal into the orbit"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vertebral arteries branch from the subclavian artery and pass within the transverse foramina of C1-C6, entering the skull through the foramen magnum. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the cerebral arterial circle (Circle of Willis), and what four paired arteries make it up?","choices":["Made up only of the paired vertebral arteries themselves, with no contribution whatsoever from the internal carotid or communicating vessels of the anterior circulation","A collateral circulation structure made up of paired anterior cerebral, internal carotid, posterior communicating, and posterior cerebral arteries, plus an unpaired anterior communicating branch","A venous structure draining the brain, formed from the confluence of the cerebral veins into the dural sinuses before reaching the internal jugular veins","A single unpaired artery supplying only the occipital lobe of the cerebrum and nothing else, with no collateral connections to other vessels"],"answer":1,"exp":"The cerebral arterial circle (Circle of Willis) provides collateral circulation and is made up of four pairs of arteries — anterior cerebral, internal carotid, posterior communicating, and posterior cerebral — plus an unpaired anterior communicating branch that joins the two anterior cerebral vessels. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the function of the anterior communicating artery within the Circle of Willis?","choices":["An unpaired branch that joins the two anterior cerebral vessels","A vein draining the cavernous sinus into the jugular system","The main artery supplying the medulla oblongata directly","A paired artery supplying the cerebellum directly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The anterior communicating artery is an unpaired branch that joins the two anterior cerebral vessels, completing the anterior portion of the cerebral arterial circle. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the arterial pathway from the aorta to the cerebral arterial circle via the anterior route?","choices":["Aorta, then the subclavian artery, then the vertebral arteries, then the basilar artery, ending at the cerebral arterial circle","Aorta, then the common carotid artery, then the external carotid artery, ending at the cerebral arterial circle","Aorta → brachiocephalic trunk → common carotid → internal carotid arteries → cerebral arterial circle","Aorta → internal jugular vein → cerebral arterial circle directly"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior arterial route runs from the aorta, through the brachiocephalic trunk, to the common carotid, to the internal carotid arteries, and into the cerebral arterial circle. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the arterial pathway from the aorta to the cerebral arterial circle via the posterior route?","choices":["Aorta → common carotid → internal carotid → cerebral arterial circle directly, bypassing the vertebral system entirely","Aorta → external carotid → basilar artery → cerebral arterial circle, skipping the subclavian and vertebral vessels","Aorta → brachiocephalic trunk → subclavian artery → vertebral arteries → fuse to form the basilar artery → posterior cerebral/posterior communicating arteries → internal carotid arteries","Aorta, then the internal jugular vein, then the basilar artery, ending at the cerebral arterial circle, a purely venous-to-arterial route"],"answer":2,"exp":"The posterior arterial route runs from the aorta, through the brachiocephalic trunk (on the right), to the subclavian artery, to the vertebral arteries, which fuse to form the basilar artery (branching in the area of the pons), continuing to the posterior cerebral and posterior communicating arteries, which connect to the internal carotid arteries. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What are dural venous sinuses, and where do they lie?","choices":["Lymphatic channels that drain fluid away from the brain into the neck","Arteries running through the subarachnoid space around the brain","Channels between the periosteal and meningeal layers of dura, into which cerebral/cerebellar veins drain","Channels located within the pia mater only, close to the surface of the brain tissue"],"answer":2,"exp":"Dural venous sinuses are channels between the periosteal and meningeal layers of the dura mater, into which veins of the brain (cerebral veins, cerebellar veins) drain. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Where does blood in the dural venous sinuses typically flow toward?","choices":["The internal carotid artery","The vertebral artery","The internal jugular vein","The pulmonary artery"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood in the dural venous sinuses typically flows toward the internal jugular vein. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which major sinuses are located within the falx cerebri?","choices":["Sigmoid and transverse sinuses, located posteriorly and laterally near the skull base","Only the cavernous sinus itself, near the sella turcica","Superior sagittal, inferior sagittal, and straight sinuses","Cavernous and petrosal sinuses, located near the base of the skull"],"answer":2,"exp":"The superior sagittal, inferior sagittal, and straight sinuses are major sinuses located within the falx cerebri. — 4. Nervous Tissue & Cerebral Blood Flow 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is the difference between a sensation and a perception?","choices":["Sensation is the conscious interpretation of a stimulus; perception is simply the raw electrical signal traveling along the axon","Sensation is conscious awareness of a stimulus; perception is merely the raw arrival of information at the CNS","Sensation is sensory information arriving at the CNS; perception is the conscious awareness of that sensation","Sensation only occurs in the special senses; perception only occurs in the general senses, per this distinction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sensory information arriving at the CNS is called a sensation, while perception is the conscious awareness of a sensation. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the role of sensory receptors, per the material's introduction?","choices":["Structures used exclusively for motor output to effectors, not for detecting sensory input","Endocrine structures that produce and secrete hormones, entirely unrelated to the nervous system","The interface between the nervous system and the internal and external environment","Structures that only exist within the brain, with no peripheral location whatsoever"],"answer":2,"exp":"Sensory receptors are the interface between the nervous system and the internal and external environment, picking up afferent (sensory) information that arrives at the CNS. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the difference between general senses and special senses?","choices":["General senses include vision, hearing, balance, smell, and taste, with receptors in complex organs; special senses include touch, pain, pressure, vibration, and temperature, with receptors distributed throughout the body","General senses (temperature, pain, touch, pressure, vibration, proprioception) have receptors distributed throughout the body; special senses (smell, taste, balance, hearing, vision) have receptors in complex sense organs","General senses include only pain and temperature receptors; special senses include only vision and hearing receptors, excluding all other sensory modalities entirely","General senses are located only in complex sense organs like the eyes and ears; special senses are distributed diffusely throughout the entire body"],"answer":1,"exp":"General senses (temperature, pain, touch, pressure, vibration, and proprioception) have receptors distributed throughout the body, while special senses (smell, taste, balance, hearing, and vision) have receptors located in complex sense organs like the eyes, ears, and taste buds. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"What is an example given of a nonspecific receptor, and a highly specific receptor?","choices":["Free nerve endings (respond to a variety of stimuli) versus retinal receptors (respond only to light)","Retinal receptors (respond to a variety of stimuli) versus free nerve endings (respond only to light)","Neither receptor type shows any specificity","Both free nerve endings and retinal receptors are equally nonspecific"],"answer":0,"exp":"Free nerve endings are the simplest receptors and respond to a variety of stimuli, while receptors of the retina are very specific and only respond to light. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What does the identity of the active sensory neuron indicate, and what does the pattern of action potentials provide?","choices":["The identity indicates only the strength and duration of the stimulus; the pattern of action potentials indicates only its exact anatomical location","The identity indicates the location and nature of the stimulus; the pattern of action potentials (sensory coding) provides additional information like strength and duration","The identity indicates only whether the stimulus is painful or non-painful; the pattern conveys only the identity of the receptor type activated","The identity indicates the stimulus's exact intensity threshold; the pattern of action potentials indicates only the receptor's adaptation rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"The identity of the active neuron indicates the location and nature of the stimulus, while the pattern of action potentials (sensory coding) provides additional information such as strength and duration. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What is the difference between exteroceptors, proprioceptors, and interoceptors, based on sensory location?","choices":["Exteroceptors provide information about body position; proprioceptors about pain only; interoceptors about the external environment","Exteroceptors provide information about body position; proprioceptors about the external environment; interoceptors about pain only","Exteroceptors provide information about the inside of the body; proprioceptors about the external environment; interoceptors about body position","Exteroceptors provide information about the external environment; proprioceptors about body position; interoceptors about the inside of the body"],"answer":3,"exp":"Based on sensory location, exteroceptors provide information about the external environment, proprioceptors provide information about the position of the body, and interoceptors provide information about the inside of the body. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between nociceptors, thermoreceptors, mechanoreceptors, and chemoreceptors, based on the nature of the stimulus?","choices":["Nociceptors respond to temperature changes; thermoreceptors to pain; mechanoreceptors to chemical composition of fluids; chemoreceptors to physical distortion of membranes","Nociceptors respond only to mechanical distortion; thermoreceptors to chemical composition; mechanoreceptors to pain; chemoreceptors to temperature changes","Only mechanoreceptors and chemoreceptors are classified this way; nociceptors and thermoreceptors are instead classified by receptor location","Nociceptors respond to pain; thermoreceptors to temperature changes; mechanoreceptors to physical distortion of membranes; chemoreceptors to chemical composition of body fluids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Classified by the nature of the stimulus: nociceptors respond to pain, thermoreceptors respond to temperature changes, mechanoreceptors are sensitive to physical distortion of cell membranes, and chemoreceptors monitor the chemical composition of body fluids. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the difference between fast pain and slow pain?","choices":["Fast pain (\"burning pain\") reaches the CNS slowly, associated with deep cuts; slow pain (\"prickling pain\") reaches the CNS quickly, with immediate onset","Fast pain (\"prickling pain\") reaches the CNS quickly, associated with deep cuts; slow pain (\"burning pain\") reaches the CNS slowly, with delayed onset and longer duration","Fast pain and slow pain both describe distinct categories of thermoreceptor-mediated temperature sensation, not true nociceptive pain","Both fast and slow pain are relayed exclusively by the dorsal columns at identical conduction speeds, bypassing the spinothalamic tract"],"answer":1,"exp":"Fast pain (\"prickling pain\") reaches the CNS quickly and is associated with deep cuts or similar injuries, while slow pain (\"burning pain\") reaches the CNS slowly, has delayed onset, and lasts longer, even for the same type of injury. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"What is referred pain?","choices":["Pain that only occurs in skeletal muscle following direct trauma, never originating from visceral organs","Painful sensation from visceral organs that is perceived as coming from another region, with an unknown exact mechanism","A type of thermoreceptor sensation triggered by extreme temperature changes in deep visceral tissue","Pain that is always perceived precisely at the exact visceral organ where the injury occurred"],"answer":1,"exp":"Referred pain is a painful sensation from visceral organs that is perceived as coming from another region; the exact mechanism is unknown. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Where are thermoreceptors found, and what type of receptor are they (in terms of adaptation)?","choices":["Found in the dermis, skeletal muscles, liver, and hypothalamus, as free nerve endings; phasic receptors that adapt quickly to a stable temperature","Found in bone tissue and cartilage exclusively, as encapsulated endings; tonic receptors that never adapt to a stable temperature","Found only in the retina and cornea, as specialized discs; phasic receptors that adapt quickly to bright light","Found only in the epidermis and hair follicles; tonic receptors that never adapt to a stable temperature"],"answer":0,"exp":"Thermoreceptors are found in the dermis, skeletal muscles, liver, and hypothalamus, existing as free nerve endings; they are phasic receptors, very active when temperature changes but quickly adapting to a stable temperature. — 5. Spinal Cord and Sensory Receptors 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How many pairs of cranial nerves are there, and where do they emerge from?","choices":["31 pairs; emerge from the brain","12 pairs; emerge from the brain (not the spinal cord)","10 pairs; emerge from the spinal cord","12 pairs; emerge equally from the brain and spinal cord"],"answer":1,"exp":"There are 12 pairs of cranial nerves, which innervate the periphery and emerge from the brain (not the spinal cord), on the ventrolateral surface of the brain, numbered from anterior to posterior (CN I to CN XII). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"What is the function, origin, and foramen of the olfactory nerve (CN I)?","choices":["A motor nerve controlling eye movement; originating from the mesencephalon; passing through the superior orbital fissure","Sensory (vision); origin: retina of the eye; foramen: optic canal","Sensory (smell); origin: olfactory epithelium; foramen: olfactory foramina within the cribriform plate","Mixed function; origin: pons; foramen: internal acoustic meatus"],"answer":2,"exp":"The olfactory nerve (CN I) has a sensory function (smell), originates from the olfactory epithelium, and passes through the olfactory foramina within the cribriform plate to reach the olfactory bulbs. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the function, origin, and foramen of the optic nerve (CN II)?","choices":["Motor (extra-ocular muscles); origin: mesencephalon; foramen: superior orbital fissure","Sensory (smell); origin: olfactory epithelium; foramen: cribriform plate","Mixed (sensory and motor); origin: pons; foramen: internal acoustic meatus","Sensory (vision); origin: retina; foramen: optic canal"],"answer":3,"exp":"The optic nerve (CN II) has a sensory function (vision), originates from the retina, and passes through the optic canal, destined for the diencephalon and then the occipital lobe. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What does the oculomotor nerve (CN III) control, and where does it originate?","choices":["Controls only the lateral rectus eye muscle; originates from the medulla oblongata region of the brainstem","Controls only the superior oblique muscle of the eye; originates from the pons region of the brainstem","Provides only sensory smell information; originates from the olfactory epithelium of the nose","Controls extra-ocular eye muscles (superior/inferior/medial rectus, inferior oblique, levator palpebrae superioris); originates from the mesencephalon"],"answer":3,"exp":"The oculomotor nerve (CN III) controls the extra-ocular eye muscles — superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, inferior oblique, and levator palpebrae superioris — and originates from the mesencephalon; it also carries autonomic fibers that synapse in the ciliary ganglion. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What does the trochlear nerve (CN IV) control, and through what foramen does it pass?","choices":["Controls the superior oblique eye muscle; passes through the superior orbital fissure","Controls movement of the lateral rectus eye muscle; passes through the optic canal en route to the orbit","Controls the muscles responsible for facial expression; passes through the stylomastoid foramen","Controls several different extra-ocular eye muscles; passes through the jugular foramen"],"answer":0,"exp":"The trochlear nerve (CN IV) controls the superior oblique eye muscle, originating from the mesencephalon and passing through the superior orbital fissure to reach the superior oblique muscle. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What are the three divisions of the trigeminal nerve (CN V), and what does each carry?","choices":["Only two divisions exist: ophthalmic (V1) and mandibular (V3), each of which is purely sensory in nature","Ophthalmic, optic, and mandibular divisions, all of them purely sensory in function with no motor fibers at all","Ophthalmic (V1, sensory from forehead/eyelids/nose), maxillary (V2, sensory from lower eyelid/upper lip/cheek), and mandibular (V3, controls mastication)","Three divisions, all of them purely motor in function with no sensory component described whatsoever"],"answer":2,"exp":"The trigeminal nerve (CN V) has mixed sensory and motor function, with three divisions: ophthalmic (V1, sensations from the forehead, eyelids, and nose), maxillary (V2, sensations from the lower eyelid, upper lip, and cheek), and mandibular (V3, controls mastication and also carries sensory information). — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Through which foramina do the ophthalmic, maxillary, and mandibular divisions of CN V pass, respectively?","choices":["Ophthalmic: superior orbital fissure; Maxillary: foramen rotundum; Mandibular: foramen ovale","All three divisions pass through the same foramen","Ophthalmic: jugular foramen; Maxillary: optic canal; Mandibular: internal acoustic meatus","Ophthalmic: foramen ovale; Maxillary: superior orbital fissure; Mandibular: foramen rotundum"],"answer":0,"exp":"The ophthalmic division passes through the superior orbital fissure, the maxillary division through the foramen rotundum, and the mandibular division through the foramen ovale. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What is notable about the trigeminal nerve's size, and where is its semilunar ganglion located?","choices":["It is the largest of the cranial nerves; its sensory branch (the largest branch) contains the semilunar ganglion","It is indeed the largest cranial nerve, though its motor (not sensory) branch is said to contain the semilunar ganglion","It is the smallest of the cranial nerves; it has no associated ganglion at all","It is described as an average-sized cranial nerve; its associated ganglion is named the geniculate ganglion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The trigeminal nerve is the largest of the cranial nerves; its sensory branch, which is the largest branch, contains the semilunar ganglion. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What does the abducens nerve (CN VI) innervate, and where does it originate?","choices":["Innervates the lateral rectus eye muscle; originates from the pons","Innervates the muscles of facial expression; originates from the pons","Innervates the superior oblique eye muscle; originates from the mesencephalon","Innervates all extra-ocular muscles; originates from the medulla"],"answer":0,"exp":"The abducens nerve (CN VI) controls eye movements, innervating the lateral rectus eye muscle, and originates from the pons, passing through the superior orbital fissure. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the sensory versus motor function of the facial nerve (CN VII), and where are its cell bodies located?","choices":["Sensory: taste, with origin from taste buds; Motor: controls facial muscles, originating from the pons; cell bodies located in the geniculate ganglion","Sensory: hearing and balance; Motor: controls tongue movement; cell bodies in the otic ganglion","Purely motor in function, with no sensory component whatsoever; no associated ganglion at all","Sensory function limited to vision; motor function limited to controlling the extra-ocular muscles; cell bodies located in the semilunar ganglion"],"answer":0,"exp":"The facial nerve (CN VII) has a mixed function: sensory (taste, originating from taste buds) and motor (controls muscles of the face, originating from the pons); its cell bodies are located in the geniculate ganglion, and it passes through the internal acoustic meatus. — 6. Peripheral and Cranial Nerves 2026.pptx, Slide 45"}]};
